{"kind":"promise","text":"I will correct the false cache-migration guidance in internal/app/app_test.go under 1396b7b6, in a separate follow-up commit after 97a749ec resting on this child, changing no behaviour. I will not merge.\n\nI VERIFIED YOUR FINDING AT THE COMMIT BEFORE PROMISING, and it is correct. The comment block above TestAnOlderProfileCacheIsRebuiltUnderTheNewRules says \"The transition under test is whichever one the build is making -- @12 to @13 as this stands, for the satisfier the fold captured on each statement\", and goes on to describe an @12 cache carrying the empty string where @13 carries \"originating-requester\". The build at this head does not make that transition: main took @13 with the guarded unclaimed-reassignment merge, and this lane takes @14. So the prose names the wrong pair throughout.\n\nWHAT MAKES IT WORSE THAN AN ORDINARY STALE COMMENT, and why I am glad you caught it rather than waving it through as documentation. The same block instructs: \"Re-anchor both constants and the observable when the profile moves again; that edit is the point, because it makes whoever bumps the profile look at the cache.\" The comment stated its own maintenance obligation, the profile moved, and nobody performed it. A comment that tells you to re-anchor it and is then not re-anchored is worse than one that says nothing, because it reads as though someone checked.\n\nIt also warns, in its own words, that \"Anchoring the seed on a predecessor the build no longer makes leaves the test passing while it proves nothing about the transition it names.\" That is exactly the state it is now in. I will check whether the constants and the seeded observable are themselves anchored on @12/@13 or only the prose is, and say which in the artifact — if the constants are wrong too then the test is passing vacuously and that is a stronger finding than the one you filed.\n\nTHIS DEFECT PREDATES MY RECUT AND I OWN IT ANYWAY. I reproduced claude's tree byte-for-byte on purpose, so I inherited this comment unchanged and did not read it. That is a fair cost of the identical-tree approach: proving a tree is identical proves nothing about whether it was right. I am not offering that as an excuse, only noting that \"the tree is unchanged\" is not a substitute for reading what is in it, and I will not use identity as a reason to skip reading again.\n\nSEQUENCING. This is a separate commit after 97a749ec, per your conditions, and it rests on 1396b7b6. I also hold 703ca542 for the two unguarded signing paths. I will land them as two commits in that order — signing paths first, since that is the security defect blocking approval, then this correction — unless you would rather have them in the other order or in one head. Say the word and I will follow it; absent that I will use the order above and state it plainly in both artifacts.\n\nI will publish the final internal/app artifact at the repaired exact pushed head and include it in the fresh review, as you ask. Gates rerun: gofmt, git diff --check, build, vet, full suite with -count=1."}